## Summary

Security review of `contracts/` at `e5e208b` using the [Pashov solidity-auditor v4](https://www.pashov.com/solidity-auditor-v4) methodology: 12 parallel attacker agents (math, access control, economics, execution trace, invariants, periphery, first principles, asymmetry, boundary, and three gap hunters), followed by dedup and four-gate validation (attack execution, reachability, unprivileged trigger, material impact). 35 files in scope; `interfaces/`, `test/`, `mocks/` and `lib/` excluded, deploy scripts included.

**Result: 10 confirmed findings, 14 leads.** All twelve agents independently converged on the same three root defects.

| # | Conf | Finding | Location |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 95 | Vote tallies keyed by recipient index the registry can shift | `BasisPointsVotingModule._processVote` |
| 2 | 95 | A one-block holder at cycle start votes with full power | `TimeWeightedVotingPower.getCurrentVotingPower` |
| 3 | 90 | Anyone applies queued recipient changes at any time | `AbstractRecipientRegistry.processQueue` |
| 4 | 85 | Dust supply lets a donation inflate the yield index past overflow | `AbstractToken._accrueGlobalYield` |
| 5 | 85 | Pool copy has the same dust-supply overflow | `AbstractStakePool._accrueGlobalYield` |
| 6 | 85 | Family path executes a removal that needs zero votes | `VotingRecipientRegistry.createCrossChainProposal` |
| 7 | 80 | `distribute` matches tallies to recipients by position only | `VotingDistributionStrategy.distribute` |
| 8 | 75 | Vote signature binds no cycle and no deadline | `AbstractVotingModule.validateSignature` |
| 9 | 75 | Dust transfers grow the checkpoint walk until a vote runs out of gas | `TimeWeightedVotingPower._calculateTimeWeightedPower` |
| 10 | 75 | The tenth vote fails when the NFT mint reverts | `VotingStreakNFTModule._updateVotingStreak` |

## The three root defects

### A. Votes are stored by array index and the recipient array can change mid-cycle (findings 1, 3, 7)

`BasisPointsVotingModule._processVote` writes `projectDistributions[cycle][i]` by registry position and the array only ever grows. `AbstractRecipientRegistry.processQueue()` is permissionless and nothing in the distribution path calls it at the cycle boundary (the README says the DM does; the code never does). A queued removal applied after votes exist makes `VotingDistributionStrategy.distribute` revert `InvalidVotesLength`, and since `startNewCycle` is only reachable from `claimAndDistribute`, the cycle freezes until the owner adds a recipient back. A remove-plus-add rebuilds the list to the same length and pays each tally to the wrong recipient. On family instances `_executeCrossChainProposal` and `applyCrossChainRegistryUpdate` call `_processQueue()` in the same tx, so no attacker is needed.

Fix options: key tallies by recipient address (rebuild the array from registry order on read), or snapshot the recipient list per cycle at the first vote; and gate `processQueue` to the distribution manager, called inside `claimAndDistribute` at the boundary.

### B. Time-weighted voting power divides by elapsed blocks, not the cycle length (findings 2, 9)

`getCurrentVotingPower` averages over `[lastCycleStartBlock, block.number)`. Deposit in the cycle-start block (or call `claimAndDistribute` and mint in the same block), vote one block later with `periodLength = 1` and full balance weight, then withdraw. The weight is frozen at cast time and never re-read. A second address can vote the same tokens again near cycle end; hopping every block sums to roughly 11× the balance over a 43,200-block cycle. The existing flash-loan test only covers a mid-cycle deposit. Separately, the checkpoint walk is unbounded and one external call per checkpoint, so ~4,500 dust transfers to a victim over a cycle make every vote entrypoint run out of gas for that victim.

Fix options: measure over the previous closed cycle, or normalise the area by `cycleLength`; bound the walk with a conservative fallback.

### C. A 1-wei first depositor can poison the yield accumulator (findings 4, 5)

`_accrueGlobalYield` adds `(raw - accounted) * 1e27 / supply` with no floor on supply. On a fresh 18-decimal instance: mint 1 wei, `setYieldSplit(10000)`, flash-loan 1e24 WXDAI into sDAI and transfer the shares in, `claimKeptYield` recovers the donation (kept = donation when supply is 1), burn and repay. `accYieldPerToken` stays around 1e51 forever; nothing resets it. Afterwards any holder with `balance * keepBps >= 1.158e26` reverts in `_attach`, and once aggregate `keepWeight` crosses the line `_donatedYieldAccrued` overflows so `yieldAccrued`, `claimYield`, `isDistributionReady`, `claimAndDistribute` and `checkUpkeep` all revert. Cost is a flash fee. Identical code in `AbstractStakePool`.

Fix: skip accrual while supply is below one whole token; the surplus stays in the donated pool.

### Also confirmed

- **Finding 6:** the cross-chain proposal path lacks the classic `requiredVotes == 0` guard, so the sole recipient (or either of two) can empty a family registry with no recovery path; donated yield is then locked on every sibling chain.
- **Finding 8:** the classic `Vote` typehash has no cycle or deadline and nonces are a set, so an unused signature (for example from a reverted submission) can be replayed in a later cycle to overwrite the voter's ballot.
- **Finding 10:** the NFT mint sits inside `_processVote` with no try/catch, so a contract voter without `onERC721Received` cannot vote in its tenth-streak cycle, and a batch containing it reverts for everyone.

## Notable leads (unscored)

- `voteWithDataBatch` lets the owner vote for every abstaining holder with no signature.
- `SexyDaiYield` / `StableYield` initializers never call `__EIP712_init`, so `delegateBySig` uses an empty domain.
- A blocklisted USDC recipient blocks every distribution (push-payment loop).
- `proposalExpiry` is read live, so the owner can revive expired proposals, contradicting the docstring.
- The "no owner lever to repoint yield" comment is contradicted by `setDistributionStrategy` / `setStrategies` / `setVotingModule`.
- `Deploy.s.sol` deploys an inert system (never calls `setYieldClaimer`, token mismatch between strategy and manager).

Admin-trust items excluded from the report by the methodology's rules but worth knowing: all ten beacons are owned by one EOA, and the strategy/voting-module setters have no timelock.

## Caveats

- The `lib/` submodules were not checked out in the audit worktree, so OpenZeppelin v5 behaviour was reasoned from documented semantics rather than the vendored source.
- AI-assisted review; it cannot prove the absence of vulnerabilities.

