[90] **3. Anyone applies queued recipient changes at any time**

`AbstractRecipientRegistry.processQueue` · Confidence: 90

**Description**
The function has no caller or timing guard, so anyone applies a queued removal mid-cycle and blocks the cycle from ending.

The README sequence says the distribution manager processes the queue at cycle end, but no contract, script or frontend hook ties `processQueue()` to the cycle boundary; the frontend `useProcessQueue` runs at any time. Combined with the index-keyed vote tally this lets any caller choose the block at which a queued removal lands. `VotingRecipientRegistry.executeProposal` is permissionless once a proposal is at threshold, so on a democratic registry the attacker also chooses when the removal is queued and can bundle `executeProposal + processQueue + claimAndDistribute` at the cycle-end block.

